From: Korvo-1 project
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc2: run the periodic scheduler on 1 ms boundaries

On the ESP32-S31 host port the SOF interrupt fires every microframe at
high speed (8 kHz).  Each SOF runs the periodic scheduler, and the
scheduled split transactions raise several channel interrupts per poll:
measured ~15 kIRQ/s total with one HID keyboard attached, which kept the
320 MHz soft-float hart at sy 60-75% and made X11 unusable.

HID-class interrupt endpoints are specified in whole milliseconds, so
run the scheduler on 1 ms boundaries only.  QH activation is due-frame
based, so endpoints simply run at the next 1 ms tick.  Forcing the whole
port to full speed instead (HCFG_FSLSSUPP) also fixes the storm, but it
caps the ESP-Hosted Wi-Fi link and any USB NIC at 12 Mbit/s and the
ESP-Hosted firmware loses association under it, so decimate here and
keep the port at high speed.

Frame numbers tick at 1 kHz in FS mode; this decimation is only correct
because the ESP32-S31 glue sets DWC2_SPEED_PARAM_HIGH.

Signed-off-by: Korvo-1 project
---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd_intr.c
@@ -125,5 +125,15 @@ static void dwc2_sof_intr(struct dwc2_hsotg *h
 	dwc2_track_missed_sofs(hsotg);

+	/*
+	 * ESP32-S31: at high speed this interrupt fires every microframe
+	 * (8 kHz) and a full periodic schedule each time costs the 320 MHz
+	 * soft-float hart more than half of its time once HID devices are
+	 * attached.  Interrupt endpoints are specified in whole
+	 * milliseconds, so schedule on 1 ms boundaries only.
+	 */
+	if (hsotg->frame_number & 7)
+		return;
+
 	/* Determine whether any periodic QHs should be executed */
 	qh_entry = hsotg->periodic_sched_inactive.next;
 	while (qh_entry != &hsotg->periodic_sched_inactive) {
